1. Find All Employees
Query
SELECT *
FROM Employee;

Output
EmpId | Name  | Salary
------+-------+--------
1     | John  | 50000
2     | Mary  | 70000
3     | Steve | 60000
4     | Tom   | 80000
5     | David | 90000

2. Find Employees With Salary > 60000
Query
SELECT Name, Salary
FROM Employee
WHERE Salary > 60000;

Output
Name  | Salary
------+--------
Mary  | 70000
Tom   | 80000
David | 90000

3. Find Distinct Departments
Query
SELECT DISTINCT DeptId
FROM Employee;

Output
10
20
30


💡 DISTINCT removes duplicate values.

4. Count Employees Per Department
Query
SELECT DeptId,
       COUNT(*) AS EmpCount
FROM Employee
GROUP BY DeptId;

Output
DeptId | EmpCount
-------+----------
10     | 2
20     | 2
30     | 1


💡 GROUP BY groups rows before applying aggregate functions.

5. Departments Having More Than 1 Employee
Query
SELECT DeptId,
       COUNT(*)
FROM Employee
GROUP BY DeptId
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

Output
DeptId
------
10
20


💡 HAVING filters groups after aggregation.

6. INNER JOIN Example
Query
SELECT e.Name,
       d.DeptName
FROM Employee e
INNER JOIN Department d
ON e.DeptId = d.DeptId;

Output
Name  | DeptName
------+----------
John  | IT
Mary  | IT
Steve | HR
Tom   | HR
David | Finance


💡 INNER JOIN returns only matching rows.

7. LEFT JOIN Example
Query
SELECT e.Name,
       d.DeptName
FROM Employee e
LEFT JOIN Department d
ON e.DeptId = d.DeptId;

Output
Name  | DeptName
------+----------
John  | IT
Mary  | IT
Steve | HR
Tom   | HR
David | Finance


💡 LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left table.

8. Average Salary Per Department
Query
SELECT DeptId,
       AVG(Salary) AS AvgSalary
FROM Employee
GROUP BY DeptId;

Output
DeptId | AvgSalary
-------+----------
10     | 60000
20     | 70000
30     | 90000

9. Employees Above Average Salary
Query
SELECT Name
FROM Employee
WHERE Salary >
(
    SELECT AVG(Salary)
    FROM Employee
);

Output
Name
-----
Tom
David


💡 Example of a Subquery.

10. Highest Salary
Query
SELECT MAX(Salary)
FROM Employee;

Output
90000

11. Second Highest Salary
Query
SELECT MAX(Salary)
FROM Employee
WHERE Salary <
(
    SELECT MAX(Salary)
    FROM Employee
);

Output
80000

12. Third Highest Salary Using ROW_NUMBER
Query
WITH X AS
(
    SELECT Salary,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER(ORDER BY Salary DESC) rn
    FROM Employee
)
SELECT Salary
FROM X
WHERE rn = 3;

Output
70000


💡 ROW_NUMBER() is a frequently asked interview topic.

13. ROW_NUMBER Example
Query
SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       ROW_NUMBER() OVER(ORDER BY Salary DESC) rn
FROM Employee;

Output
Name  | Salary | rn
------+--------+----
David | 90000  | 1
Tom   | 80000  | 2
Mary  | 70000  | 3
Steve | 60000  | 4
John  | 50000  | 5

14. RANK Example
Query
SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       RANK() OVER(ORDER BY Salary DESC) rnk
FROM Employee;

Output
Name  | Salary | Rank
------+--------+------
David | 90000  | 1
Tom   | 80000  | 2
Mary  | 70000  | 3
Steve | 60000  | 4
John  | 50000  | 5


💡 RANK() leaves gaps when ties occur.

15. Running Total
Query
SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       SUM(Salary)
       OVER(ORDER BY Salary) AS RunningTotal
FROM Employee;

Output
Name  | Salary | RunningTotal
------+--------+--------------
John  | 50000  | 50000
Steve | 60000  | 110000
Mary  | 70000  | 180000
Tom   | 80000  | 260000
David | 90000  | 350000


💡 Running totals are common window function questions.

16. Department Average Using PARTITION BY
Query
SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       AVG(Salary)
       OVER(PARTITION BY DeptId) AS DeptAvg
FROM Employee;

Output
Name  | Salary | DeptAvg
------+--------+---------
John  | 50000  | 60000
Mary  | 70000  | 60000
Steve | 60000  | 70000
Tom   | 80000  | 70000
David | 90000  | 90000


💡 PARTITION BY calculates group metrics while keeping detail rows.

17. Duplicate Records
Query
SELECT Email,
       COUNT(*)
FROM Customer
GROUP BY Email
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

Output
Duplicate Emails Found


💡 Very common production support interview question.

18. UNION vs UNION ALL
UNION
SELECT Name FROM Employee
UNION
SELECT Name FROM Customer;

Output
Duplicates Removed

UNION ALL
SELECT Name FROM Employee
UNION ALL
SELECT Name FROM Customer;

Output
Duplicates Retained

19. Find Employees Without Department
Query
SELECT e.*
FROM Employee e
LEFT JOIN Department d
ON e.DeptId = d.DeptId
WHERE d.DeptId IS NULL;

Output
Unmatched Employees


💡 Useful for data-quality and support issues.

20. Top 3 Highest Salaries
Query
SELECT Salary
FROM Employee
ORDER BY Salary DESC
FETCH FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY;

Output
90000
80000
70000


💡 Modern SQL equivalent of TOP 3 / LIMIT 3.

